# What the build context excludes, and the two things it must not exclude.
#
# This file and deploy/Dockerfile disagreed, and the disagreement was a broken
# image rather than a slow one. The Dockerfile copies `tests/` (so the
# repository contract can be run against the container's own PostgreSQL) and
# `data/embeddings.db` (so a first build reuses the shipped vectors instead of
# re-embedding 552 passages inside a CPU container). Both were excluded here,
# so `COPY` failed on a path the context did not contain:
#
#     failed to compute cache key: "/tests": not found
#
# An exclusion that makes a COPY fail is not a smaller image, it is no image.
# Anything added to this file that the Dockerfile copies has to be re-admitted
# below with a `!` rule, and the CI container job is what proves it.

.git
.gitignore
.venv
__pycache__
*.pyc
.pytest_cache
.mypy_cache
*.egg-info
dist
build
.DS_Store
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
node_modules
.idea
.vscode
*.md
!README.md
.github
.claude/

# Databases are excluded as a class, because the built index is a machine-local
# artefact tied to one embedding model and copying it in would ship an index the
# container must rebuild anyway.
*.sqlite
*.db
data/index/

# The exception, and the reason the class rule above is not simply wrong: the
# embedding cache is content-addressed by text, model tag and dimension, so a
# vector comes back only for the exact text the exact model computed it from.
# It is a lookup table that either hits or misses, never a stale index --
# DECISIONS 17. Shipping it turns a first container build from about fifteen
# minutes of CPU embedding into seconds.
!data/embeddings.db
